WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.590
 Marvel Studios Loki
has introduced us

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.330
to all kinds of wild
characters, which

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.250
is why I'm speaking with
VFX Supervisor Brad Parker.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:10.050
Hey, Brad.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:10.850
 Hey there.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.050
 Now, there are a ton
of incredible characters

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.180
that you and your team got
to work on for the series,

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.030
but I want to talk about one
of my favorites, Miss Minutes.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:19.740
How would you describe
her on the show?

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:20.540
 Sure.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.550
So Miss Minutes is
this digital mascot

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.120
who appears to kind of
provide story exposition,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.880
to talk to characters, to give
them information that they

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.600
might not normally have who kind
of pops up any time she's sort

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.790
of needed or wanted,
or not wanted,

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.940
as it can turn out to
be from time to time.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.750
She was a really interesting
creation in that she wanted

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.670
to look almost hand animated.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.560
She has almost a retro style.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.060
But she's fully
three dimensional,

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.340
like a contemporary hologram.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.950
The visual artists that
we worked with on this

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.440
created a series
of tests of what

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.090
this clock person could look
like dimensionalized into 3D.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:04.019
And then we also did a series of
tests of what her overall look

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:05.129
or patina might be.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:07.230
You know, she's got
these sort of scan lines

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:08.920
that travel through her body.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.650
She's semi-transparent.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.870
All of those little
elements and decisions

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:16.140
were things that were
painstakingly tested and tested

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.260
again until we arrived
at sort of a recipe

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.870
for what it is that
Miss Minutes looks like.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:23.430
 So once you have
that recipe, how

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.410
do you take the scene
filled with real actors

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.880
and add a digital character?

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.910
 When you're working with
real actors who are interacting

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.740
with characters
that aren't there,

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.140
you generally want to make
sure that the real actors

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.410
know where to look and where the
other character is going to be.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.260
In that case, we provide
what's called eyelines.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.540
That could be as simple as
a tennis ball on a stick

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:43.710
that the actors look at.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.610
In the case of Miss Minutes,
this was often a little LED

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.880
lamp that would create a
little bit of a kick of light

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.820
that she emanates from
herself onto the walls.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.800
The actors were able
to look at that lamp

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.420
and know that's
where Miss Minutes

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.750
would be at any given moment.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.510
 Well, speaking of blending
the real and the fantastic,

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.220
let's talk about the
Time-Keepers, who are

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.560
also very important to the TVA.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.180
Could you talk us
through that scene

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.600
where we first get to see
the Time-Keepers in action?

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:14.400
 Sure.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.910
So the Time-Keepers were
particularly interesting

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:18.110
in that they're all digital.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:22.280
They're meant to be these sort
of lizard-like anthropomorphic

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.520
creatures that control
the flow of time

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.500
and protect the sacred timeline.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.930
Now they're entirely
digitally created.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.100
They're hand animated.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:33.110
And they're based on
a vocal performance

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:35.390
by a single actor, which
is pretty impressive.

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:37.280
We soon discover
that they actually

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.000
are in fact robots
or androids and not

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.390
living, breathing things.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.210
So it was an
interesting challenge

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:47.390
to kind of create performances
that felt like robots

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:50.480
pretending to be lizard
creatures that were

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.500
ultimately kind of found out.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:53.300
 All right.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.580
That is layers on
layers on layers.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.500
But once you have
all of that, how

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.600
do you take them from
being more humanoid

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.280
and making them more robotic?

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.650
 In order to create
the Time-Keepers,

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:06.350
digital sculptors
will create a version

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:08.540
of a digital [INAUDIBLE]
or, you know, small

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.170
sculpture in the computer.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.530
We take it to the next step,
which is adding texture.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:14.270
Making sure the
cloth looks real,

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:18.410
that the material flows nicely
and has the proper amount

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:22.130
of shine, and that their skin
looks the way that it needs to,

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:22.940
and their eyes.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:24.590
And all of those
things are developed

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:27.320
by talented visual
artists in the computer.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.820
We then animate
their performance.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.690
This was a case where we
didn't do performance capture.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:37.370
Animators hand animate all the
movements of the Time-Keepers.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:40.070
Ultimately, everything is
rendered into the scene,

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.380
and compositors
piece it all together

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.840
and create one seamless effect.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.330
 Well, it's all been
such a sight to behold.

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:50.750
Now, looking back at the
entire series, what does it

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:53.780
mean to you as an artist
to be a part of the Marvel

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.010
Cinematic Universe?

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:59.090
 It means a lot as a digital
artist to kind of contribute

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:02.690
to the Marvel Universe and
introduce new characters,

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:05.060
like Miss Minutes
and the Time-Keepers.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:09.350
And to be able to identify
what they look like

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:12.650
and how they behave and
basically who they are

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.590
is really exciting because
you know that that work is

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:17.430
going to live on in the future.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:18.950
 Thank you so much, Brad.

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.920
And of course, everyone, go
experience Marvel Studios'

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.200
Loki, only on Disney Plus.

